 "standard_url": "https://www.thecorestandards.org/Math/Content/HSN/CN/B/4/",
 "standard_text": "Represent complex numbers on the complex plane in rectangular and polar form (including real and imaginary numbers), and explain why the rectangular and polar forms of a given complex number represent the same number.",
 "meta_desc": "Free precalculus worksheet on complex numbers in polar form: modulus and argument, converting forms, multiplying, dividing, De Moivre's theorem and the complex plane. 100 questions.",
 "key_big": "z = r(cos θ + i sin θ)",
 "key_sub": "Multiply: multiply the moduli and add the angles. Powers: rⁿ and nθ.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><ul><li>Plot <i>a</i> + <i>bi</i> at (<i>a</i>, <i>b</i>). Its <b>modulus</b> is |<i>z</i>| = √(<i>a</i>² + <i>b</i>²), the distance from 0.</li><li>Its <b>argument</b> θ is the angle from the positive real axis. Find the reference angle with tan⁻¹|<i>b</i>/<i>a</i>| and then use the quadrant.</li><li><b>Polar form</b>: <i>z</i> = <i>r</i>(cos θ + <i>i</i> sin θ). Back to rectangular: <i>a</i> = <i>r</i> cos θ, <i>b</i> = <i>r</i> sin θ.</li><li>To multiply, multiply the moduli and add the angles. To divide, divide and subtract. <b>De Moivre</b>: [<i>r</i>(cos θ + <i>i</i> sin θ)]<sup><i>n</i></sup> = <i>r</i><sup><i>n</i></sup>(cos <i>n</i>θ + <i>i</i> sin <i>n</i>θ).</li></ul><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Write −2 + 2√3<i>i</i> in polar form.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>r</i> = √(4 + 12) = 4</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Reference angle 60°; Quadrant II, so θ = 120°</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 4(cos 120° + <i>i</i> sin 120°)</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find [2(cos 30° + <i>i</i> sin 30°)]<sup>4</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<sup>4</sup> = 16</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>4 × 30° = 120°</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 16(cos 120° + <i>i</i> sin 120°)</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Adding the moduli when multiplying. Multiply them.</li><li>Multiplying the angles when multiplying numbers. Add them.</li><li>Using tan⁻¹(<i>b</i>/<i>a</i>) directly in Quadrants II and III. Check where the point is.</li><li>Forgetting the square root: |3 + 4<i>i</i>| is 5, not 25.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the polar form of a complex number?",
   "a": "z = r(cos θ + i sin θ), where r is the distance from 0 (the modulus) and θ is the angle from the positive real axis (the argument)."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you multiply complex numbers in polar form?",
   "a": "Multiply the moduli and add the arguments. To divide, divide the moduli and subtract the arguments."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is De Moivre's theorem?",
   "a": "[r(cos θ + i sin θ)]ⁿ = rⁿ(cos nθ + i sin nθ): raise the modulus to the power and multiply the angle by n."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you find the distance between two complex numbers?",
   "a": "Subtract them and take the modulus: |z − w|. It is the same as the distance formula for the points."
  }
 ],
